entry gamma-radiation
term: Gamma Radiation
related_terms: gamma-ray
related_terms: electromagnetic-radiation
related_terms: decay
related_terms: radioactivity
related_terms: radiation
quick_facts: It is electromagnetic radiation, not a particle
quick_facts: It is like light, but far more energetic
quick_facts: It carries away extra energy from the nucleus
quick_facts: The nucleus keeps the same protons and neutrons
quick_facts: It is not made of tiny pieces of the nucleus

sources:
    {"organization":"OpenStax","title":"University Physics Volume 3: Radioactive Decay","url":"https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-3/pages/10-3-radioactive-decay"}

definition:
    High-energy electromagnetic radiation given off by a nucleus, usually right after an alpha or beta decay leaves it with extra energy. Gamma radiation is not a particle and is not made of pieces of the nucleus. It is like light, but far more energetic. Giving off gamma radiation changes neither the number of protons nor the number of neutrons.

debunking_snippet:
    “Gamma radiation is made of tiny pieces of the nucleus.” It is electromagnetic radiation, like light but far more energetic.
